      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Inhaled corticosteroids do not suppress inflammation or control symptoms in all asthmatics. In particular, corticosteroid insensitivity exists in many patients and may potentially be reversible. There is a need to develop new anti-inflammatory therapies for this disease. This article critically reviews clinical trial data of novel anti-inflammatory drugs in asthma, encompassing specific anti-eosinophil therapies, including antisense chemokine receptor antagonists, anti-cytokine monoclonal antibodies and small-molecule approaches. We provide an insight into the possible future of asthma therapy by identifying drugs with the most promising therapeutic profile."},
